Aster OS test disk

Deterministic image built by tools/make-test-disk.sh (GPT + ext2 read-only, ^dir_index).

The disk carries the user-facing configuration surface for the desktop shell.
The whole shell is written in Lua and lives inside the kernel; these files
customize it at runtime (loaded from the disk, applied live).

/wm/ directory (window manager configuration)
  theme.lua    colors and geometry (hot-reloadable, see below)
  api.lua      the Lua API reference the shell exposes
  .theme.bak   manual backup of the last Ctrl+S (read-only, never loaded)

Hot reload
  Edit /wm/theme.lua and save it (Ctrl+S in the editor, Super+Z opens it) —
  the desktop repaints immediately, no F5 and no kernel rebuild. If the new
  theme does not parse or run, the change is reported in the REPL and the
  shell keeps the built-in defaults; the system never crashes. To restore a
  previous save, view .theme.bak (Space in the file browser) and copy its
  content back into theme.lua manually — backups are never loaded
  automatically. Deleting /wm/theme.lua and /wm/.theme.bak is safe — the
  shell falls back to the built-in defaults, so the environment never breaks.

Using the shell
  The WM follows Hyprland conventions — shortcuts, buttons and navigation
  behave the same as in Hyprland.

  Super+T  editor: fresh empty buffer each time. Arrows move the cursor/line,
           Ctrl+S saves (an unsaved buffer asks for a path and the file is
           created), unsaved edits survive Super+T, Esc Esc closes the editor
           when there are no unsaved changes.
  Super+Z  settings: opens /wm/theme.lua; saving it repaints the shell live.
  Super+E  file browser at the root: Up/Down select, Enter opens/edits a
           regular file, Space previews, Delete removes it, Escape or a click
           on the title bar goes up a level. Hidden dotfiles are shown dim,
           read-only files like .theme.bak in red. F2 renames the selected
           entry, Shift+F4 starts a new file, and a file saved from the editor
           appears in the listing right away.
  Super+Space  launcher; its "help" entry lists every shortcut. F1 inside a
           window shows that app's cheat sheet, Super+F1 (anywhere) the global
           WM help; Alt+Tab cycles windows.

  Deletion is permanent for now — /.trash exists as the future trash location
  (empty-trash is planned, not wired yet).

Changing window behaviour
  theme.lua controls colors and geometry (what the shell draws). To change
  which windows exist, the keybindings, the launcher entries or the taskbar,
  the WM exposes its Lua API — see api.lua and the shell source modules
  (wm.lua, input.lua, launcher.lua) for the hooks you can call from Lua.
